# 5. What Problems Security & Compliance Addresses

Security & Compliance should be understood through the security and governance problems it helps organizations address.

## 5.1 Security exposure across production environments

Organizations need visibility into where meaningful security exposure exists and how that exposure relates to the systems, identities, and infrastructure supporting the environment.

Security & Compliance can help assess those risks and support appropriate remediation.

## 5.2 Compliance and framework requirements

Organizations may need to demonstrate alignment with external security expectations or industry frameworks such as TPN.

Security & Compliance can support:

* readiness assessment
* control alignment
* documentation
* remediation planning
* ongoing compliance-related work

Compliance should be treated as one part of the security environment rather than as proof that the organization is fully secure.

## 5.3 Access and identity governance

Security increasingly depends on understanding who or what has access, what authority that access carries, and whether it remains appropriate as the environment changes.

This can involve human identities, application identities, service accounts, vendors, or other forms of authorized access.

The specific identity model should be discussed only when relevant to the article.

## 5.4 Vulnerability visibility and remediation

Organizations may have incomplete visibility into vulnerabilities across the infrastructure and systems they operate.

Security & Compliance can help identify exposure, assess its significance, and support remediation based on the actual environment.

## 5.5 Security across connected or distributed environments

Security boundaries can extend across systems, locations, cloud environments, and external relationships.

The security problem is therefore often broader than an individual endpoint or application.

Content in this area should focus on the specific trust, access, control, or visibility issue rather than repeatedly describing the environment as complex.

## 5.6 Security controls that need to remain operationally viable

Security controls have to function inside the environment they are intended to protect.

Production awareness matters when implementation choices affect how systems, users, or workflows actually operate.

This should not be reduced to the generic claim that “security must balance protection and productivity.” Articles should identify the specific control or operating condition that creates the tension.

